## Implementation Details

### Graph State Model
- Represent each state as an ordered pair `(a, b)` where `a` and `b` are distinct image indices
- Implement adjacency check: `states[i]` and `states[i+1]` are adjacent if they share exactly one image index
- Store walk as array of state objects: `[{a: 0, b: 1}, {a: 1, b: 2}, ..., {a: n, b: 0}]`

### Closed Walk Generation Algorithm
```javascript
// Core algorithm implementation
class ClosedWalkGenerator {
  constructor(imageIndices, loopLength) {
    this.images = imageIndices;
    this.loopLength = loopLength;
    this.graph = this.buildAdjacencyGraph();
  }

  buildAdjacencyGraph() {
    // Create adjacency list for all valid state pairs
    const states = [];
    const adjacency = new Map();

    // Generate all possible ordered pairs (a, b) where a !== b
    for (let i = 0; i < this.images.length; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < this.images.length; j++) {
        if (i !== j) {
          const stateKey = `${i},${j}`;
          states.push({a: i, b: j});
          adjacency.set(stateKey, []);
        }
      }
    }

    // Build adjacency relationships
    states.forEach(state => {
      const key = `${state.a},${state.b}`;
      states.forEach(neighbor => {
        if (this.areAdjacent(state, neighbor)) {
          adjacency.get(key).push(neighbor);
        }
      });
    });

    return { states, adjacency };
  }

  areAdjacent(state1, state2) {
    // Share exactly one image index
    const shareA = state1.a === state2.a || state1.a === state2.b;
    const shareB = state1.b === state2.a || state1.b === state2.b;
    return (shareA !== shareB) && (state1.a !== state1.b) && (state2.a !== state2.b);
  }

  generateClosedWalk() {
    const { states, adjacency } = this.graph;
    const startState = this.getRandomState(states);
    const walk = [startState];
    const visited = new Set();
    visited.add(this.getStateKey(startState));

    // Build walk using backtracking with heuristics
    for (let i = 1; i < this.loopLength; i++) {
      const current = walk[walk.length - 1];
      const neighbors = adjacency.get(this.getStateKey(current));
      const validNext = neighbors.filter(n => !visited.has(this.getStateKey(n)));

      const next = validNext.length > 0 
        ? this.selectBestTransition(current, validNext)
        : this.getRandomState(neighbors);

      walk.push(next);
      visited.add(this.getStateKey(next));
    }

    // Ensure loop closure - final state must be adjacent to start
    if (!this.areAdjacent(walk[walk.length - 1], startState)) {
      // Find bridging state or regenerate
      return this.generateClosedWalk(); // Recursive retry
    }

    return walk;
  }

  selectBestTransition(current, candidates) {
    // Heuristic: prefer transitions with visual similarity
    // This is a placeholder for image similarity metrics
    return candidates[Math.floor(Math.random() * candidates.length)];
  }

  getStateKey(state) {
    return `${state.a},${state.b}`;
  }

  getRandomState(states) {
    return states[Math.floor(Math.random() * states.length)];
  }
}
```

### Validation & Edge Cases
- Pre-validate: `loopLength <= (n * (n-1))` where n = number of images
- Handle insufficient images: if n < 3, return error
- Implement max retry attempts (10) for walk generation
- Fallback: generate shortest possible loop if requested length impossible

### Integration Points
- Accept filtered image array from existing theme/filter system
- Return walk sequence in format: `[{frame: 0, pair: {a: idx, b: idx}}, ...]`
- Expose `validateLoopLength(images, length)` method for UI validation

### Performance Optimization
- Cache adjacency graph for repeated generations with same image set
- Use Web Workers for graph generation with large image sets (>100 images)
- Implement incremental walk building to avoid full recalculation on parameter changes

### State Management
- Store generated walk in animation state store
- Provide `regenerateWalk()` method for user-triggered regeneration
- Include metadata: generation timestamp, image count, loop length, success status

